    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d48">

                <pb facs="0058" n="46" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg46"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.20 Defense/58⅜: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-10T15:00:00-03:00">June 10, 1940—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3:11 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>259. The German and Russian and notably the Russian broadcasts are talking a lot
                    about our alleged imperialistic designs in Latin <pb facs="0059" n="47"
                        xml:id="pg47"/> America under cover of acquiring air bases; and the Germans
                    are stressing the trips of Colonel Miller and Captain Spears.</p>
                <p>The local Germans are attempting to tie up the approaching visits of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Quincy</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic">Wichita</hi>
                    with all that.<anchor n="61" xml:id="d48-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d48-f1" n="61" xml:id="d48-f1-content">
                <p>See section entitled “Concern of the United States over Nazi activities in
                    Uruguay; visits of United States naval vessels to the eastern coast of South
                    America’ <ref target="#pg1147">pp. 1147</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
